module Formatter.Audit.DecisionTrace exposing
    ( Decision
    , DeclTrace
    , Flip
    , diffDeclarations
    , reportToJson
    , traceDeclarations
    )

{-| Which layout decisions the formatter made, and whether it makes the same
ones again.

This module backs the CLI's `--decisions` flag:

    gren-format --decisions MyFile.gren

which formats a file twice and prints, as JSON, the layout decisions that
differed between the two passes. It is a debugging aid for **non-idempotent
formatting** -- output that changes when the formatter is run on its own
output. It sits off to the side of the pipeline; nothing in `Formatter.Logical`
or `Formatter.Render` depends on it.


## Why it exists

Every other check in this repo compares *output*. When a format is not a fixed
point, a byte diff shows **where** the two runs disagree but never **why**, so
the decision behind each diff has to be reconstructed by hand, one diff at a
time -- and two diffs with the same cause look no more alike than two with
different ones.

This module gives them a shared vocabulary. It reads back, per top-level
declaration, the layout decisions the formatter took, as `name = choice` pairs.
A diff like

    -   foo : Int -> {- c -} { a : Int, b : String }
    +   foo :
    +       Int
    +       -> {- c -} { a : Int, b : String }

comes back as

    decl 3 `foo`  AcrossOrVertical.forceVertical  False x1 -> True x1

and a decision name is something many findings can share, so a pile of diffs
becomes a histogram instead of a reading list. `tests/check-decision-stability.py`
is the driver that does exactly that over the fixture corpus; see
`docs/testing.md` for how to run it and how to read the groups it prints.


## How a trace is built and compared

`traceDeclarations` walks a finished LPT and returns one `DeclTrace` per
top-level declaration: the decisions taken inside it, plus the text it renders
to. `diffDeclarations` compares two such arrays -- one per formatting pass --
and returns the `Flip`s, the branches whose population changed. `reportToJson`
renders the result as the `--decisions` payload.

Three properties of that comparison are deliberate:

  - **Decisions carry no source positions.** A row is precisely what moves
    between two formats, so a decision keyed on one would flag every finding and
    explain none. A `Decision` records only the declaration it was taken in, the
    decision's name, and the branch taken.

  - **Traces are compared as multisets of `(decl, name, choice)`**, not aligned
    node by node. A comment that lands under a different parent on the reparse
    shifts every structural path after it, and an alignment that reports forty
    spurious moves for one real flip is worse than no alignment at all. A
    multiset difference reports exactly the branches whose population changed.

  - **Only declarations whose rendered output moved are compared.** Formatting a
    file that is not already canonical legitimately changes many decisions --
    the second pass reads the first pass's tidied rows, not the author's -- so
    an unrestricted comparison is mostly the formatter converging. What the
    restriction leaves out is counted in `convergedFlips` rather than dropped
    silently.


## What may be traced: trace an input, never a composite

Two kinds of value are traced, and **nothing here is recomputed from a formula
written in this module**:

  - **Flags the LPT already carries** -- `forceVertical`,
    `checkContentVertical`, the bracket-container constructor, each comment's
    `CommentRole`, and the blank lines `VerticalSpace` put above a declaration.
    These are the author-intent decisions, taken in the logical stage from the
    source rows, and they are read straight off the node.

  - **Results of calling the renderer's own exported predicates** with the
    node's own children -- `commentBreaksFlowRow`, `literalCommentsRideFlatLine`,
    `rnTypeSegmentsBroken`, `commentEndsItsLine`, `commentTextCanRide` --
    plus, for the shapes whose layout is decided by measuring a rendered child,
    whether that child's box comes back on one line (`makePBox` +
    `isSingleLine`, the same question `Formatter.Audit.PredicateAgreement`
    asks).

Composite decisions are deliberately left out. `commentForcesBracketOpen`, for
example, is a genuine layout decision, but tracing it would mean copying its
formula out of `MakeRenderBox` -- a mirror, free to drift from the original and
then report confidently about code that no longer behaves that way. Its two
inputs are traced instead, so a flip in it still shows up, under the name of
whichever input moved.

The cost of that rule is coverage: a decision with no traced input means some
byte diffs will show no flipped decision at all. That is reported rather than
hidden -- a diff with an empty flip list comes back as `unexplained`, and the
count of those is this module's own to-do list. Adding a traced decision is how
it comes down; guessing at one is how the report starts lying.

@docs Decision, DeclTrace, Flip, traceDeclarations, diffDeclarations, reportToJson

-}

import Formatter.Logical.LogicalPrintingTree exposing
    ( LPNode
    , LPShape(..)
    , SyntaxType(..)
    , bracketsToString
    , roleToString
    , stypeToString
    )
import Formatter.Render as Render
import Formatter.Render.BoxOps exposing (isSingleLine)
import Formatter.Render.MakeRenderBox exposing (makePBox)
import Formatter.Render.NodeClassify exposing
    ( commentBreaksFlowRow
    , commentEndsItsLine
    , commentTextCanRide
    , literalCommentsRideFlatLine
    )
import Formatter.RenderTree exposing
    ( RenderNode
    , lower
    , rnChildren
    , rnShape
    , rnTypeSegmentsBroken
    )
import Json.Encode as Json


{-| One decision: the declaration it was taken in, its name, and the branch.

`decl` is `"<n> <name>"` -- the declaration's index among the root's *declaration*
children (comments and blank lines are not counted, so inserting one does not
renumber anything) and the first literal token in it, which for every
declaration kind is the name being declared.

`choice` is a short enum-ish string (`"True"`, `"LeadsLine"`,
`"alwaysVertical:curly"`), never a rendering and never a number that could
drift.
-}
type alias Decision =
    { decl : String
    , name : String
    , choice : String
    }


{-| One branch whose population changed between the two formats: it was taken
`before` times in the first format and `after` times in the second, and the two
differ. A flip normally arrives as a pair -- `False 1 -> 0` beside
`True 0 -> 1` -- because the node is still there and took the other branch; a
lone entry means the decision stopped (or started) being asked at all, which is
a structural change and worth reading differently.
-}
type alias Flip =
    { decl : String
    , name : String
    , choice : String
    , before : Int
    , after : Int
    }


-- TRACING


{-| One declaration's decisions, beside the text it renders to.

The text is what keeps the diff useful: `diffDeclarations` compares decisions
only in declarations whose rendered output moved, so every flip it reports is a
decision that had a visible effect. Without that restriction the report is
mostly the formatter converging on a file that was not canonical to begin with.
-}
type alias DeclTrace =
    { decl : String
    , decisions : Array Decision
    , text : String
    }


{-| The trace, grouped by declaration, each carrying the text it renders to.

Root children are attributed to a declaration: a declaration's own subtree to
itself, and a leading comment or blank line to the declaration it precedes
(`"eof"` for anything trailing the last one), because that is the code it was
written against.

Attribution puts a declaration's leading comments and blank lines under its
label, so the labels come out in contiguous runs and the grouping is a single
pass. The text of a run is its children's rendered output joined the way
`renderRoot` joins it, so two runs compare exactly as the two files do.
-}
traceDeclarations : LPNode -> Result String (Array DeclTrace)
traceDeclarations root =
    let
        -- Lowered ONCE and used twice: the render below and the decision walk
        -- read the same tree. `Render.renderLoweredChildren` exists for exactly
        -- this -- see its docstring.
        rendered =
            lower root
    in
    Render.renderLoweredChildren rendered
        |> Result.map
            (\texts ->
                rnChildren rendered
                    |> labelRootChildren
                    |> attributeToFollowing
                    |> Array.indexedMap
                        (\index item ->
                            { decl = item.decl
                            , decisions = rootChildDecisions item
                            , text = Maybe.withDefault "" (Array.get index texts)
                            }
                        )
                    |> Array.foldl
                        (\entry acc ->
                            when Array.popLast acc is
                                Just { last = prev, initial = rest } ->
                                    if prev.decl == entry.decl then
                                        Array.pushLast
                                            { prev
                                                | decisions = prev.decisions ++ entry.decisions
                                                , text = prev.text ++ "\n" ++ entry.text
                                            }
                                            rest

                                    else
                                        Array.pushLast entry acc

                                Nothing ->
                                    Array.pushLast entry acc
                        )
                        []
            )


{-| A root child's own label, if it is a declaration, plus how many blank lines
`VerticalSpace` put directly above it. -}
type alias LabelledChild =
    { node : RenderNode
    , ownLabel : Maybe String
    , blanksAbove : Int
    }


labelRootChildren : Array RenderNode -> Array LabelledChild
labelRootChildren kids =
    (Array.foldl
        (\node acc ->
            if isEmptyLineNode node then
                -- Counted, and still emitted: the result stays index-aligned
                -- with `rnChildren`, which is what lets `traceDeclarations`
                -- pair each child with its rendered text.
                { acc
                    | blanks = acc.blanks + 1
                    , out = Array.pushLast { node = node, ownLabel = Nothing, blanksAbove = 0 } acc.out
                }

            else if isDeclarationChild node then
                { declIndex = acc.declIndex + 1
                , blanks = 0
                , out =
                    Array.pushLast
                        { node = node
                        , ownLabel = Just (String.fromInt acc.declIndex ++ " " ++ declarationName node)
                        , blanksAbove = acc.blanks
                        }
                        acc.out
                }

            else
                { acc
                    | blanks = 0
                    , out = Array.pushLast { node = node, ownLabel = Nothing, blanksAbove = 0 } acc.out
                }
        )
        { declIndex = 1, blanks = 0, out = [] }
        kids
    ).out


{-| Give every non-declaration root child the label of the declaration it leads.
Right-to-left, so each one sees the next declaration's label. -}
attributeToFollowing : Array LabelledChild -> Array { node : RenderNode, decl : String, ownLabel : Maybe String, blanksAbove : Int }
attributeToFollowing items =
    (Array.foldr
        (\item acc ->
            let
                label =
                    Maybe.withDefault acc.next item.ownLabel
            in
            { next = label
            , out =
                Array.pushFirst
                    { node = item.node
                    , decl = label
                    , ownLabel = item.ownLabel
                    , blanksAbove = item.blanksAbove
                    }
                    acc.out
            }
        )
        { next = "eof", out = [] }
        items
    ).out


rootChildDecisions : { node : RenderNode, decl : String, ownLabel : Maybe String, blanksAbove : Int } -> Array Decision
rootChildDecisions item =
    let
        blankLines =
            when item.ownLabel is
                Just _ ->
                    [ { decl = item.decl
                      , name = "VerticalSpace.blankLinesAbove"
                      , choice = String.fromInt item.blanksAbove
                      }
                    ]

                Nothing ->
                    []
    in
    blankLines ++ collect item.decl item.node


collect : String -> RenderNode -> Array Decision
collect decl node =
    nodeDecisions decl node ++ Array.mapAndFlatten (collect decl) (rnChildren node)


{-| The decisions visible at one node. See the module doc for the rule that
decides what belongs here. -}
nodeDecisions : String -> RenderNode -> Array Decision
nodeDecisions decl node =
    let
        children =
            rnChildren node

        say name choice =
            { decl = decl, name = name, choice = choice }

        flag name value =
            say name (boolName value)

        -- Whether this node's own box comes back on one line. Every "render the
        -- child and look at it" decision in `MakeRenderBox` reads exactly this,
        -- so tracing it covers all of them at once without reproducing any of
        -- them. `Err` is a real third outcome: a node whose builder needs
        -- context its parent holds cannot be rendered alone, and saying so is
        -- better than picking a boolean.
        shape prefix =
            when makePBox (rnShape node) children is
                Ok box ->
                    [ say (prefix ++ ".rendersOneLine") (boolName (isSingleLine box)) ]

                Err _ ->
                    [ say (prefix ++ ".rendersOneLine") "notRenderable" ]
    in
    when rnShape node is
        AcrossOrVertical r ->
            [ flag "AcrossOrVertical.forceVertical" r.forceVertical
            , flag "AcrossOrVertical.checkContentVertical" r.checkContentVertical
            , flag "AcrossOrVertical.commentBreaksFlowRow" (commentBreaksFlowRow children)
            ]
                ++ shape "AcrossOrVertical"

        ParenBlock r ->
            [ flag "ParenBlock.forceVertical" r.forceVertical
            , flag "ParenBlock.checkContentVertical" r.checkContentVertical
            , flag "ParenBlock.contentAlwaysBreaks" r.contentAlwaysBreaks
            ]
                ++ shape "ParenBlock"

        Binop r ->
            [ flag "Binop.forceVertical" r.forceVertical ] ++ shape "Binop"

        Pipeline r ->
            [ flag "Pipeline.forceVertical" r.forceVertical ] ++ shape "Pipeline"

        IfCondition r ->
            [ flag "IfCondition.forceVertical" r.forceVertical ] ++ shape "IfCondition"

        WhenFlow r ->
            [ flag "WhenFlow.forceVertical" r.forceVertical ] ++ shape "WhenFlow"

        RecordUpdate r ->
            [ flag "RecordUpdate.forceVertical" r.forceVertical
            , flag "RecordUpdate.literalCommentsRideFlatLine" (literalCommentsRideFlatLine children)
            ]
                ++ shape "RecordUpdate"

        AllAcrossOrAllVertical brackets ->
            [ say "BracketContainer.shape" ("allAcrossOrAllVertical:" ++ bracketsToString brackets)
            , flag "BracketContainer.literalCommentsRideFlatLine" (literalCommentsRideFlatLine children)
            ]
                ++ shape "BracketContainer"

        AlwaysVertical brackets ->
            [ say "BracketContainer.shape" ("alwaysVertical:" ++ bracketsToString brackets)
            , flag "BracketContainer.literalCommentsRideFlatLine" (literalCommentsRideFlatLine children)
            ]

        EmptyBracketed brackets ->
            [ say "BracketContainer.shape" ("empty:" ++ bracketsToString brackets.value) ]

        SingleLineComment c ->
            [ say "Comment.role" (roleToString c.role)
            , flag "Comment.endsItsLine" (commentEndsItsLine node)
            , flag "Comment.textCanRide" (commentTextCanRide node)
            ]

        BlockComment c ->
            [ say "Comment.role" (roleToString c.role)
            , flag "Comment.endsItsLine" (commentEndsItsLine node)
            , flag "Comment.textCanRide" (commentTextCanRide node)
            ]

        OriginalRows r ->
            when r.stype is
                StFunctionSignature ->
                    -- The decision NAME is kept, though the function behind it
                    -- moved to `Formatter.RenderTree` and was renamed there: it
                    -- is an output key, and this module's own `boxKind` note
                    -- says why those do not follow a rename.
                    [ flag "Signature.signatureForceVertical" (rnTypeSegmentsBroken node) ]

                StPort ->
                    [ flag "Signature.signatureForceVertical" (rnTypeSegmentsBroken node) ]

                _ ->
                    []

        PipelineStep _ ->
            shape "PipelineStep"

        IndentedBlock ->
            shape "IndentedBlock"

        SoftIndentedBlock ->
            shape "SoftIndentedBlock"

        BodyBlock ->
            shape "BodyBlock"

        OpAndRhs ->
            shape "OpAndRhs"

        _ ->
            []


isEmptyLineNode : RenderNode -> Bool
isEmptyLineNode node =
    rnShape node == EmptyLine


{-| A root child that is a real declaration rather than a detached comment or a
blank line. Only these are counted when numbering declarations, so a comment
moving in or out of the root does not renumber the ones after it. -}
isDeclarationChild : RenderNode -> Bool
isDeclarationChild node =
    when rnShape node is
        OriginalRows r ->
            when r.stype is
                StSingleLineComment ->
                    False

                StBlockComment ->
                    False

                StDocComment ->
                    False

                _ ->
                    True

        _ ->
            False


{-| The declared name: the first literal token in the declaration's subtree. For
every `SyntaxType` that reaches here that is the name being declared (`module`
and `import` name themselves after their keyword, which is synthesized and so
skipped). Used only as a label, alongside the index that actually keys it. -}
declarationName : RenderNode -> String
declarationName node =
    Maybe.withDefault (stypeToString (declStype node)) (firstLiteralToken node)


declStype : RenderNode -> SyntaxType
declStype node =
    when rnShape node is
        OriginalRows r ->
            r.stype

        _ ->
            StFunctionDeclaration


firstLiteralToken : RenderNode -> Maybe String
firstLiteralToken node =
    when rnShape node is
        UnbreakableText loc ->
            Just loc.value

        _ ->
            Array.foldl
                (\child acc ->
                    when acc is
                        Just _ ->
                            acc

                        Nothing ->
                            firstLiteralToken child
                )
                Nothing
                (rnChildren node)


boolName : Bool -> String
boolName b =
    if b then
        "True"

    else
        "False"


-- DIFFING


{-| The branches whose population differs between two traces.

A multiset difference, not an alignment -- see the module doc. Both sides are
tallied by `(decl, name, choice)` and merged; a key present on one side only
reports the other as `0`.
-}
diffTraces : Array Decision -> Array Decision -> Array Flip
diffTraces before after =
    mergeTallies (tally before) (tally after) 0 0 []


{-| What changed between two formats, with the decisions that did nothing left
out.

`flips` covers only the declarations whose rendered output differs, so every
entry is a decision that had a visible effect. `convergedFlips` counts the rest:
decisions that differ inside a declaration whose output is byte-identical. Those
are not findings -- they are what a second format legitimately decides
differently once the first pass has tidied the rows those flags are read from --
but the number is reported anyway, so the size of what is being filtered out
stays visible. `movedDecls` names the declarations that moved.

Declarations are aligned by position. Formatting neither adds nor removes one,
and the index counts only real declarations (`isDeclarationChild`), so the
alignment survives any amount of comment and blank-line movement; a pair whose
labels disagree is reported as moved rather than skipped.
-}
diffDeclarations :
    Array DeclTrace
    -> Array DeclTrace
    -> { flips : Array Flip, movedDecls : Array String, convergedFlips : Int }
diffDeclarations before after =
    let
        walked =
            walkDecls before after 0 { movedBefore = [], movedAfter = [], movedDecls = [], converged = 0 }
    in
    { flips = diffTraces walked.movedBefore walked.movedAfter
    , movedDecls = walked.movedDecls
    , convergedFlips = walked.converged
    }


type alias DeclDiffAcc =
    { movedBefore : Array Decision
    , movedAfter : Array Decision
    , movedDecls : Array String
    , converged : Int
    }


walkDecls : Array DeclTrace -> Array DeclTrace -> Int -> DeclDiffAcc -> DeclDiffAcc
walkDecls before after i acc =
    when { b = Array.get i before, a = Array.get i after } is
        { b = Nothing, a = Nothing } ->
            acc

        { b = Just bd, a = Just ad } ->
            if bd.decl == ad.decl && bd.text == ad.text then
                walkDecls before
                    after
                    (i + 1)
                    { acc | converged = acc.converged + Array.length (diffTraces bd.decisions ad.decisions) }

            else
                walkDecls before
                    after
                    (i + 1)
                    { acc
                        | movedBefore = acc.movedBefore ++ bd.decisions
                        , movedAfter = acc.movedAfter ++ ad.decisions
                        , movedDecls =
                            Array.pushLast
                                (if bd.decl == ad.decl then
                                    bd.decl

                                 else
                                    bd.decl ++ " -> " ++ ad.decl
                                )
                                acc.movedDecls
                    }

        { b = Just bd, a = Nothing } ->
            walkDecls before
                after
                (i + 1)
                { acc
                    | movedBefore = acc.movedBefore ++ bd.decisions
                    , movedDecls = Array.pushLast (bd.decl ++ " (gone)") acc.movedDecls
                }

        { b = Nothing, a = Just ad } ->
            walkDecls before
                after
                (i + 1)
                { acc
                    | movedAfter = acc.movedAfter ++ ad.decisions
                    , movedDecls = Array.pushLast (ad.decl ++ " (new)") acc.movedDecls
                }


type alias Tally =
    { decl : String
    , name : String
    , choice : String
    , sortKey : String
    , count : Int
    }


tally : Array Decision -> Array Tally
tally decisions =
    decisions
        |> Array.map (\d -> { decl = d.decl, name = d.name, choice = d.choice, sortKey = sortKeyOf d, count = 1 })
        |> Array.sortBy .sortKey
        |> Array.foldl
            (\entry acc ->
                when Array.popLast acc is
                    Just { last = prev, initial = rest } ->
                        if prev.sortKey == entry.sortKey then
                            Array.pushLast { prev | count = prev.count + 1 } rest

                        else
                            Array.pushLast entry acc

                    Nothing ->
                        Array.pushLast entry acc
            )
            []


{-| The tally key. `\u{0001}` cannot occur in a declaration name, a decision
name or a choice, so the packed string orders and compares exactly as the triple
does. -}
sortKeyOf : Decision -> String
sortKeyOf d =
    d.decl ++ "\u{0001}" ++ d.name ++ "\u{0001}" ++ d.choice


{-| Merge two key-sorted tallies, keeping only the keys whose counts differ.
Index-walked and tail-recursive: a large file has tens of thousands of
decisions. -}
mergeTallies : Array Tally -> Array Tally -> Int -> Int -> Array Flip -> Array Flip
mergeTallies before after i j acc =
    when { b = Array.get i before, a = Array.get j after } is
        { b = Nothing, a = Nothing } ->
            acc

        { b = Just bEntry, a = Nothing } ->
            mergeTallies before after (i + 1) j (Array.pushLast (flipOf bEntry bEntry.count 0) acc)

        { b = Nothing, a = Just aEntry } ->
            mergeTallies before after i (j + 1) (Array.pushLast (flipOf aEntry 0 aEntry.count) acc)

        { b = Just bEntry, a = Just aEntry } ->
            if bEntry.sortKey == aEntry.sortKey then
                if bEntry.count == aEntry.count then
                    mergeTallies before after (i + 1) (j + 1) acc

                else
                    mergeTallies before after (i + 1) (j + 1) (Array.pushLast (flipOf bEntry bEntry.count aEntry.count) acc)

            else if bEntry.sortKey < aEntry.sortKey then
                mergeTallies before after (i + 1) j (Array.pushLast (flipOf bEntry bEntry.count 0) acc)

            else
                mergeTallies before after i (j + 1) (Array.pushLast (flipOf aEntry 0 aEntry.count) acc)


flipOf : Tally -> Int -> Int -> Flip
flipOf entry before after =
    { decl = entry.decl
    , name = entry.name
    , choice = entry.choice
    , before = before
    , after = after
    }


-- REPORTING


{-| Encode the result as the JSON payload `--decisions` prints.

The field worth knowing is `unexplained`: true when the two formats produced
different bytes and yet no traced decision changed, meaning the branch that
moved is one this module does not record. It is reported per file rather than
summed across a run, because fixing it is per-file work -- find the missing
decision, trace it (under the rule in the module doc), and the file drops off
the list.
-}
reportToJson :
    { bytesDiffer : Bool
    , flips : Array Flip
    , movedDecls : Array String
    , convergedFlips : Int
    , decisionCount : Int
    }
    -> String
reportToJson report =
    Json.object
        [ { key = "bytesDiffer", value = Json.bool report.bytesDiffer }
        , { key = "unexplained", value = Json.bool (report.bytesDiffer && Array.isEmpty report.flips) }
        , { key = "decisionCount", value = Json.int report.decisionCount }
        , { key = "convergedFlips", value = Json.int report.convergedFlips }
        , { key = "movedDecls", value = Json.array Json.string report.movedDecls }
        , { key = "flips"
          , value =
                Json.array
                    (\f ->
                        Json.object
                            [ { key = "decl", value = Json.string f.decl }
                            , { key = "name", value = Json.string f.name }
                            , { key = "choice", value = Json.string f.choice }
                            , { key = "before", value = Json.int f.before }
                            , { key = "after", value = Json.int f.after }
                            ]
                    )
                    report.flips
          }
        ]
        |> Json.encode 2
